{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786518108,"nonce":"n4LgmovkS0Sv6B2SRiq1XoPyBaI=","title":"**What is wrong.** cc0fac2 fixed `orgLister.Scopes`'s missing `deleted_at IS NULL` filter and made it page, but its own `Done when` had a second clause that never shipped: *\"the fan-outs that walk it enqueue per-tenant jobs instead of looping every tenant inside one handler.\"* Six job kinds still call `Scopes(ctx)` and loop the result synchronously inside a single job execution, exactly as before cc0fac2: `internal/app/workers.go:150` (`forEachOrg`, shared by `reapOccurrences`/`occurrence.reap`, `closeGroups`/`group.close`, `scoreFlaps`/`flap.score` — three job kinds off one loop), `:302` (`effectiveRetention`, called from `managePartitions`/`partitions.manage`), `:375` (`sweepDrills`, called from `pruneRetention`/`retention.prune`), and `internal/stats/worker/rollup.go:56` (`StatsRollup`/`stats.rollup`). That is `occurrence.reap`, `group.close`, `flap.score`, `partitions.manage`, `retention.prune` and `stats.rollup` — every one of them doing O(tenants) sequential work inside one execution, against one timeout, on one worker goroutine.","message":"**The demonstration.** `internal/app/workers.go:140-142`: *\"sweepLimit bounds one tick's work per tenant. A sweep that is not bounded is a sweep that becomes an outage the first time somebody has a bad night.\"* `sweepLimit = 500` (`:142`) caps rows per org inside `reapOccurrences`, `closeGroups`, `scoreFlaps` and `sweepDrills` — but nothing caps the number of orgs one execution visits, and the timeouts are fixed regardless of tenant count. `internal/platform/jobs/registry.go` pairs each: `OccurrenceReap` ticks every minute (`:268-270`) with a 2-minute execution timeout (`:202-204`); `GroupClose` the same pairing (`:271-273`, `:205-208`); `FlapScore` ticks every 5 minutes, same 2-minute timeout (`:274-276`, `:209-212`); `RetentionPrune` and `PartitionsManage` tick hourly with a 10-minute timeout each (`:283-285`/`:221-224`, `:280-282`/`:217-220`); `StatsRollup` ticks every 15 minutes with a 5-minute timeout (`:292-294`, `:225-228`). Adding a tenant adds fixed work to all six ticks inside the same fixed timeout window — a budget that is being spent on tenant count, a variable nobody bounded.\n\n**Why it matters.** A per-row bound does not bound tenants-per-execution. cc0fac2 gestured at `Reconciler.FanOut` as the shape the other periodics should copy, but it is not a usable precedent: `FanOut` (`internal/sources/service/reconcile.go:637-657`) itself loops `r.orgs.Scopes(ctx)` synchronously at `:642`, and what it enqueues is scoped per-SOURCE, never per-tenant — `fanOutTenant` (`:660-687`) calls `r.enq.EnqueueMany` with `jobs.SourceReconcileArgs{SourceID: ...}` and `jobs.SilencesSyncArgs{SourceID: ...}` (`:671-681`), both keyed on a source id. There is also no existing per-tenant job args type to model a fix on: none of the fifteen args structs in `internal/platform/jobs/args.go` carries an `OrgID` field (verified by search — zero matches). A per-tenant `occurrence.reap` job is new surface, not a wiring change.\n\nTwo of the six do not decompose into that shape at all, which is why this is not a mechanical fan-out. `effectiveRetention` (`internal/app/workers.go:295-326`) is a REDUCE: it computes ONE maximum retention window across every tenant to drive a single `oto_partitions_manage` call (`managePartitionsSQL`, `:249`, invoked at `:270`). There is no per-tenant unit of work to enqueue — the fan-out pattern does not apply until something decides how a reduce becomes \"one job per tenant\" (accumulate N async results? keep it synchronous but bound tenant count some other way?). `pruneRetention` (`:334-352`) interleaves a global `ingest_dedup` prune (`c.Ingestion.Service.PruneDedup`, `:337`), a global, `sweepLimit`-bounded session sweep (`c.Identity.SweepExpiredSessions`, `:341`), and the per-org `sweepDrills` (`:345`) inside one job body. Splitting the org loop out means either `retention.prune` keeps its two global prunes and hands only `sweepDrills` to a new fan-out, or the job kind gets restructured outright.\n\nA known, already-accepted consequence of cc0fac2 belongs on record here too, corrected to what actually happens rather than assumed: because `sweepDrills` (`workers.go:371-392`) now skips soft-deleted tenants, a departed tenant's open drills are never finalised and their synthetic rows are never disposed of by `Service.Sweep` (`internal/drill/service/service.go:276-314`) again. Those rows do **not**, in general, age out through `partitions.manage` — `docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:39-41` lists `alerts`, `alert_occurrences`, `alert_groups`, `notifications`, `notification_deliveries` and `channel_threads` as *\"Never reaped, by anything, at any setting.\"* `internal/drill/repository/dispose.go:50-122` is, by its own comment (`:21`), *\"the ONLY function in oto that deletes a signal row\"* — it is what removes a drill's rows from exactly those tables, and it is what a departed tenant no longer gets. The one exception is `alert_events`: it is the single table in that set `partitions.manage` actually drops on a schedule (ADR 0024 §1), and `Dispose` deletes a drill's `alert_events` rows explicitly by id anyway (`dispose.go:66-77`) rather than waiting for that drop. The `delivery_drills` receipt row is unaffected either way — it is designed to outlive disposal forever (`db/migrations/00039_delivery_drills.sql:52`, *\"WHAT SURVIVES DISPOSAL IS THE RECEIPT\"*). So the accurate statement is: a departed tenant's drill-manufactured alert/group/occurrence/thread/notification rows now persist indefinitely with no reaper reaching them, not that they age out elsewhere. Accepted as a consequence of cc0fac2 for now; worth revisiting alongside whatever this ticket decides.\n\n**Where.** `internal/app/workers.go:140-142` (the bound that doesn't cover tenant count), `:149-163` (`forEachOrg`), `:295-326` (`effectiveRetention`), `:334-352` (`pruneRetention`), `:371-392` (`sweepDrills`); `internal/stats/worker/rollup.go:42-82` (`StatsRollup`); `internal/platform/jobs/registry.go:202-228` (timeouts), `:268-294` (tick intervals); `internal/platform/jobs/args.go` (no `OrgID`-bearing args type exists); `internal/sources/service/reconcile.go:637-687` (`FanOut`/`fanOutTenant`, the disqualified precedent); `internal/drill/repository/dispose.go:21,50-122` and `docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:39-41` (the drill-sweep consequence).\n\n**Scope.** large. Six job kinds to convert, no existing per-tenant args type and no existing enqueue-per-org precedent to copy (the one candidate is disqualified above), plus two of the six needing an explicit design decision before any fan-out can be written for them at all. That spans `internal/app/workers.go`, `internal/stats/worker/rollup.go`, `internal/platform/jobs/args.go` and `registry.go`, `internal/app/container.go` (wiring), `internal/sources/service/reconcile.go` if its shape is reused for anything, and the test files for every one of those. Two independent estimates made during triage put this at 350-500+ LOC across 7+ files; nothing found while verifying this ticket suggests it is smaller.\n\n**Done when.** `effectiveRetention` and `pruneRetention` each have a written decision for how, or whether, they fan out — not silently forced into the same per-tenant-job shape as the other four — and the four that do decompose cleanly (`occurrence.reap`, `group.close`, `flap.score`, `stats.rollup`) enqueue one job per tenant from a bounded fan-out tick instead of looping `Scopes()` inside one execution. The drill-sweep consequence above is at minimum re-stated as a known, accepted gap in whatever design doc or follow-up this produces, since it is presently undocumented anywhere outside this ticket.","files":null}]}